e minutes to move a seed packet to the broadcast tower. Three minutes to change the world."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Remembering', 'text': "By dawn the Patrol had stood down — human, after all, and shown the same thirty seconds. the Consortium was gone by noon, the Algorithm's servers dark. the Glass District belonged to its people again. It had only ever been on loan.\n\na seed packet burned out in the broadcast — one use, that was the price. Nyx Carter held the dead chip and didn't mourn it. Seeds die to grow. That was the whole point.\n\nWhat happened in the next hour, historians argue about. What Nyx Carter remembers: silence, then a sound like a held breath released, then the streets filling with people who had remembered they were people."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'After the Static', 'text': 'Nyx Carter kept the dead chip in a pocket. A reminder: that truth is a seed, that seeds die to grow, that one a seed packet in the right hands at the right time is worth an army.\n\nAnd when the new council asked Nyx Carter to lead, Nyx Carter laughed and went back to the food lines. Leaders, Nyx Carter had learned, are just people who show up. Nyx Carter shows up.\n\nRebuilding the Glass District was harder than freeing it. Nyx Carter organized food lines with the same care once used for heists. the runner Pax taught kids to code on salvaged terminals. The medic ran a clinic. The runner ran messages of hope.'}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Habit of Freedom', 'text': "the runner Pax runs the network now — the real one, the open one, the one that belongs to everyone. The checkpoint is quieter. The neon is warmer. It turns out freedom has a sound, and it's laughter.\n\nAnd somewhere, in a gutter where drones don't look, someone is finding something warm that shouldn't be warm. It starts again. It always starts again. That's the best part.\n\nSometimes Nyx Carter walks to the tower and looks out over the Glass District, green in the cracks of the concrete, alive in the way of things that remember. a seed packet is gone. What it carried isn't."}]}

